keywords:
  - large language models
  - model-as-a-service
  - service-level monitoring
  - API brokerage
  - behavioral consistency
  - energy statistics
abstract: >-
  Organizations routing production workloads through aggregated large language model (LLM) brokers rely on declared
  model identifiers, yet the backend serving each request remains hidden. We present HuePrint, a black-box framework
  that audits brokered endpoint consistency via structured color elicitation and geometric comparison without weights
  or token probabilities. Across 38,688 cloud responses (56 endpoints, 12 backends), HuePrint cuts 30-call/side equal
  error rates (EERs) over word baselines from 4.52-5.23 to 0.94-1.00 percent on development models and 6.25-8.04 to
  3.49-5.00 percent across 20 locked endpoints (0.00 percent at 60-150 calls/side). On those 20 endpoints, it averages
  5.18 percent within-family EER across ten balanced 30-call splits (0.00-7.89 percent), beating LLMmap (10.53-34.39
  percent) and cost-matched continuation kernels (11.09-19.47 percent); relative to LLMmap, it also avoids
  safety-triggering probes and runs over 15,000 times faster locally. Regularized RGB energy lowers shallow EER
  (5.36 vs. 10.71 percent exact hex and 6.42 percent string MMD), while hybrid scoring cuts cross-provider EER from
  16.67 to 5.93 percent. In replay, two-stage confirmation cuts false alerts from 14 to 1 across 100 same-label
  windows and flags 186/190 within-family swaps under one cent.
